      "sha256": "6590fe46db28eb2dae7bf86e53a158fcb3cb99e8b3c86424e27d794e1a1af301",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 5: Fixations of Participant 11 and 14. Participant 11 (left) tracked the animation at a high speed closely following the code as it became visible. Participant 14 (right) played the animation slowly, reading other parts of the computer code."
    },
    {
      "path": "figures/spinelli-attention-patterns-for-code-animations-px182018/figure-006-p004.png",
      "node": "471:0",
      "page": 4,
      "bbox": [
        315.9549865722656,
        265.33917236328125,
        560.1983032226562,
        445.22601318359375
      ],
      "sha256": "4842beb5f8df64933034cdad23f8f904426a5a15f5670f8d9f0fbbe918bf0f4e",
      "kind": "figure",
      "alt": "Figure 6. Box plots of mean K-coefficient values for four participants by activity and treatment: linear animation (blue), non-linear animation (green), and static code (orange). For summarization, linear and non-linear animation are higher than static. For syntax errors, animation values are mostly positive while static values are near or below zero. For logic errors, static has the highest central value, non-linear is near zero, and linear has the broadest spread extending well below zero. Higher K indicates more focal attention; lower K indicates more ambient attention."
    }
